Experiment 1: Analysis of Signals in time domain
1.   Aim: To study the analysis of signals in time-domain and understand the concepts of sampling,
     convolution and correlation.


2.   Software used: MATLAB


3.   Theory:
          Time-domain analysis is the study of a signal by observing how its amplitude varies with time.
     It is one of the fundamental approaches in digital signal processing and provides insight into the
     behavior and characteristics of signals before they are analyzed in other domains. Most real-
     world signals, such as speech, audio, and biomedical signals, are initially examined in the time
     domain to understand their temporal properties.

        Digital signal processing operates on discrete-time signals, which are obtained by sampling
     continuous-time signals at regular intervals. Sampling is the process of converting an analog
     signal into a sequence of discrete samples while preserving the essential information of the
     original signal. Proper selection of the sampling frequency is necessary to accurately
     reconstruct the original signal and avoid information loss.

         Convolution is a fundamental mathematical operation used to determine the output of a
     linear time-invariant (LTI) system for a given input signal and system impulse response. It
     describes how the characteristics of a system modify the input signal. Correlation is another
     important operation that measures the degree of similarity between two signals or between
     different segments of the same signal. It is widely used in applications such as pattern
     recognition, synchronization, signal detection, and communication systems.

     Understanding these concepts is essential for analyzing and processing digital signals and
     forms the foundation for advanced topics such as digital filter design, frequency-domain
     analysis, and real-time signal processing.



