Experiment 2: Analysis of Signals and Systems in Z-domain
1. Aim: To study the analysis of signals and systems in Z-domain


2. Software used: MATLAB


3.   Theory:
The z-transform is a fundamental mathematical tool used in digital signal processing to analyze
discrete-time signals and systems in the complex frequency domain. It provides a convenient
method for representing and solving linear difference equations, determining system characteristics,
and analyzing the stability and frequency response of discrete-time systems. The z-transform of a
discrete-time signal x[n] is defined as
                                                     ∞

                                         𝑋(𝑧) =      ∑ 𝑥[𝑛] 𝑧 −𝑛 ,
                                                  𝑛= −∞

where z is a complex variable. The set of values of z for which the z-transform converges is known
as the Region of Convergence (ROC), which plays an important role in determining the existence,
causality, and stability of a system.

The transfer function of a discrete-time system is expressed as the ratio of the output to the input in
                                              𝑌(𝑧)
the z-domain and is represented as 𝐻(𝑧) = 𝑋(𝑧). The locations of poles and zeros of the transfer
function provide valuable information about the system's behavior. A system is considered stable if
all its poles lie inside the unit circle in the z-plane, while the frequency response of the system can
be obtained by evaluating the transfer function on the unit circle. The z-transform simplifies the
analysis of discrete-time systems by converting convolution operations in the time domain into
multiplication in the z-domain, making it an indispensable tool in the design and analysis of digital
filters and other DSP applications.



