Experiment 3: Analysis of Signals and Systems in Frequency
                               domain


1. Aim: To study the frequency-domain representation and analysis of signals and systems.


2. Software used: MATLAB


3.   Theory:
     Frequency-domain analysis is a fundamental technique in digital signal processing used to
examine the frequency components present in a signal. While time-domain analysis describes how a
signal varies with time, frequency-domain analysis reveals how the signal's energy or power is
distributed across different frequencies. This representation is particularly useful for understanding
the spectral characteristics of signals and the behavior of digital systems.

    The transformation from the time domain to the frequency domain is achieved using the
Discrete Fourier Transform (DFT), which expresses a finite-length discrete-time signal as a sum of
sinusoidal components of different frequencies. The Fast Fourier Transform (FFT) is an efficient
algorithm for computing the DFT, significantly reducing the computational complexity. The
magnitude spectrum indicates the strength of various frequency components, while the phase
spectrum provides information about their phase relationships.

     The frequency response of a discrete-time system describes its effect on different frequency
components of the input signal. It is obtained by evaluating the system's transfer function on the unit
circle in the z-plane. Frequency-domain analysis is extensively used in digital filter design, spectrum
analysis, communication systems, speech and image processing, biomedical signal analysis, and
many other signal processing applications. A clear understanding of the frequency-domain
representation of signals and systems is essential for analyzing, designing, and implementing
efficient digital signal processing algorithms.



