3.   Theory:
Digital image processing involves manipulation and analysis of digital images to improve their
quality or extract useful information. A digital image is represented as a two-dimensional
array of pixels, where each pixel corresponds to an intensity or color value. Image processing
techniques are widely used in applications such as medical imaging, remote sensing,
surveillance, industrial inspection, computer vision, and multimedia systems.

Basic image processing operations include image acquisition, grayscale conversion,
histogram analysis, intensity transformations, filtering, and geometric transformations.
Histogram analysis provides information about the distribution of pixel intensities and is
useful for evaluating image contrast. Histogram equalization enhances image contrast by
redistributing intensity levels, while negative, logarithmic, and gamma transformations modify
image brightness and contrast for improved visualization. Spatial filtering techniques, such as
mean, Gaussian, and median filtering, are used to smooth images, reduce noise, or preserve
important image features. Other operations, including cropping, resizing, and color
component extraction, enable the manipulation and analysis of specific image regions or
color information. These fundamental image processing techniques provide the basis for
advanced applications such as object recognition, image segmentation, compression, and
computer vision.



